

> When two subjects/things are thought of as **a single unit**, the article is used before the **first noun only**.

### Structure:

> **A/AN + Noun + and + Noun**

---

## Example 1 — Cup and Saucer

> **He was ready with a cup and a saucer.**

If **cup and saucer** are considered as one combined unit, use:

> **He was ready with a cup and saucer.** ✅

The article **a** is used only before the first noun.

---

## Example 2 — Singer and Scientist

Compare:

> **He introduced me to his friend who is a singer and a scientist.**

Here the two nouns may be treated separately.

But when **singer and scientist** describe the **same person as one combined identity**, Rani Ma'am's rule gives:

> **He introduced me to his friend who is a singer and scientist.** ✅

The article **a** is used only before the first noun.

---

### ⭐ Important

Do **not** blindly remove the second article.

Ask:

> Are the two nouns being considered as **one unit/one identity**?

### One combined unit:

> **a cup and saucer**

### Two separate things:

> **a cup and a saucer**

---

### One person with two roles:

> **a singer and scientist**

### Two separate identities:

> **a singer and a scientist**

